---
description: Allow proof-first development and documented, encapsulated Java/JS/TS
alwaysApply: true
---

- Before changing production behavior, supplement the JSON and Lean contract and
  compile its new obligations. Preserve previous verification evidence.
- For every authored Java/JS/TS method, constructor, function and callback:
  provide meaningful Javadoc/JSDoc and keep its declaration/body within 30
  physical lines. Do not evade the limit with dense one-line statements.
- Document parameters, return values, side effects and relevant errors. Use named
  documented helpers instead of undocumented inline callback logic.
- Java classes encapsulate fields: private final by default, immutable records
  only for deliberate value DTOs, defensive copies for mutable values.
  JS/TS classes use private state; exports expose minimal typed interfaces.
- Follow single responsibility, explicit dependency injection, clear names,
  bounded inputs and precise failures. Never swallow exceptions.
- Canonical and embedding data contain resolved anonymous slots and structural
  values only. Display names, source, comments, paths and name maps belong to
  a separate presentation/provenance boundary.
- Reject unsupported canonical constructs; never fall back to raw source text.
- Treat local corpus access as permission for local development only. Keep
  rights, redistribution, training and remote embeddings separately gated.
